1
00:00:24,274 --> 00:00:26,109
We are Maya Ongaku

2
00:00:26,109 --> 00:00:27,360
our music is

3
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,279
psychodelic, ambient

4
00:00:29,279 --> 00:00:31,281
like folk music

5
00:00:32,365 --> 00:00:33,408
and pop.

6
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:37,954
Enoshima is a city that is facing the sea

7
00:00:37,954 --> 00:00:42,834
and it's a lot like Portugal, with plenty of nature.

8
00:00:42,834 --> 00:00:46,129
and the ocean, and lots of interesting people

9
00:00:46,129 --> 00:00:49,340
so we live in a really fun environment.

10
00:00:49,674 --> 00:00:50,842
For us,

11
00:00:50,842 --> 00:00:53,094
that influence of nature

12
00:00:53,094 --> 00:00:55,013
because of the sea

13
00:00:55,013 --> 00:00:59,517
We don't have the impression that we do this kind of music

14
00:00:59,517 --> 00:01:05,064
but people often tell us they can really feel the waves in our music,

15
00:01:05,064 --> 00:01:10,278
and how that is said frequently,

16
00:01:10,278 --> 00:01:13,656
certainly, since come from a town by the sea,

17
00:01:13,656 --> 00:01:15,825
maybe that's might be true.

18
00:01:57,534 --> 00:01:58,451
In Japan,

19
00:01:58,451 --> 00:02:04,666
bands going on tour in America or Europe

20
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:07,794
is considered extremely difficult.
é considerado difícil

21
00:02:07,794 --> 00:02:15,927
We got lucky to get opportunities to go to America and Europe

22
00:02:15,927 --> 00:02:18,972
And what we realized there was

23
00:02:18,972 --> 00:02:23,977
rather than saying it's not that difficult,

24
00:02:23,977 --> 00:02:25,812
 it's not easy, but

25
00:02:25,812 --> 00:02:27,730
if you work hard and put in the effort,

26
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,108
it's possible, that's the level of difficulty.

27
00:02:30,108 --> 00:02:34,320
Given that this information isn't generally known,

28
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,906
considering that
considerando isso

29
00:02:36,906 --> 00:02:41,369
we think we should share it.
transmitir

30
00:02:41,369 --> 00:02:46,082
So, since last year, we've been keeping a tour diary

31
00:02:46,082 --> 00:02:51,838
to show what overseas touring is actually like,

32
00:02:51,838 --> 00:02:55,842
how difficult it is and how much money it costs,

33
00:02:58,219 --> 00:03:03,933
By making that information public for the next generation of musicians,

34
00:03:04,392 --> 00:03:07,478
or even not only musicians, but artists too

35
00:03:07,478 --> 00:03:09,022
we want people in Japan to be able to

36
00:03:09,022 --> 00:03:12,275
make their way overseas

37
00:03:12,609 --> 00:03:16,404
and create a path

38
00:03:16,529 --> 00:03:17,572
that's what we're doing.

39
00:03:17,572 --> 00:03:20,450
For example, our senior

40
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:21,993
DYGL

41
00:03:22,744 --> 00:03:24,370
the band called DYGL,

42
00:03:26,623 --> 00:03:28,207
came directly to us

43
00:03:28,207 --> 00:03:32,378
asked us how we tour,

44
00:03:32,378 --> 00:03:34,631
and asked us for information.

45
00:03:34,631 --> 00:03:35,715
And they

46
00:03:35,715 --> 00:03:39,302
successfully did a European tour this year

47
00:04:07,789 --> 00:04:09,582
But overseas,

48
00:04:09,582 --> 00:04:11,417
it feels as if you're performing the concert together.

49
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:13,461
The audience makes some noise,

50
00:04:13,461 --> 00:04:15,254
and we make music,

51
00:04:15,254 --> 00:04:16,130
and those things

52
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:19,300
That style of live performance

53
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:22,428
was unusual for us, having played in Japan.

54
00:04:22,428 --> 00:04:24,013
I think that's a major difference.

55
00:04:24,097 --> 00:04:24,973
In Japan,

56
00:04:24,973 --> 00:04:28,226
people go to see the musicians

57
00:04:28,851 --> 00:04:30,937
But in Europe and America,

58
00:04:31,062 --> 00:04:32,438
people go to hear music.

59
00:04:32,438 --> 00:04:35,608
So it doesn't matter who is making the music,

60
00:04:36,025 --> 00:04:37,151
but in Japan, that would never happen.

61
00:04:37,443 --> 00:04:41,531
Today (for example), to go and see a band that whose name you don't know

62
00:04:41,531 --> 00:04:42,991
that happens in Europe and America

63
00:04:42,991 --> 00:04:44,492
That doesn't really happen in Japan.

64
00:05:23,865 --> 00:05:27,118
The way we got in touch with Guruguru Brain

65
00:05:27,118 --> 00:05:32,331
It happened via social media

66
00:05:32,331 --> 00:05:37,420
I was publishing on SoundCloud tracks like "Experimental Series"

67
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:39,714
There were about 1, 2, 3 and 4 of those tracks.

68
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:43,468
Then they happened to hear by chance

69
00:05:43,468 --> 00:05:47,805
Then the people from Guruguru Brain sent an Instagram DM

70
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:54,979
Since we were already huge fans of them, we were like "Here it comes!"

71
00:05:54,979 --> 00:06:01,611
Then we created a group chatm then it started

72
00:06:01,611 --> 00:06:08,367
"Do you want to go on an European Tour and make a record?"

73
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:13,706
We were like "Yes, of course we want to!"

74
00:06:13,706 --> 00:06:18,836
But even before that, I'd been going to their love shows for a long time. 

75
00:06:18,836 --> 00:06:21,214
I have been a fan for a long time!

76
00:06:50,201 --> 00:06:53,913
(Traveling to) Brazil was "Muito bom", ie. very good.

77
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:57,375
Why did I go to Brazil?

78
00:06:57,375 --> 00:07:02,213
It was a Musical Journey

79
00:07:02,213 --> 00:07:09,387
I brought my flute and my saxophone, but I hadn't anything decided.

80
00:07:09,387 --> 00:07:12,557
I first arrived in São Paulo

81
00:07:12,557 --> 00:07:16,352
But first, through a person introduced by Guruguru Brain

82
00:07:16,352 --> 00:07:21,232
I connected with a Brazilian Bossa Nova artist

83
00:07:21,232 --> 00:07:26,571
I spent a week with that person, playing music together and so on

84
00:07:26,571 --> 00:07:30,199
We did a live performance

85
00:07:30,199 --> 00:07:34,162
Then I went to Florianópolis

86
00:07:34,162 --> 00:07:39,250
There I meet a Brazilian fluent in Japanese

87
00:07:39,250 --> 00:07:44,130
Then I went to Salvador, Bahia

88
00:07:44,130 --> 00:07:48,885
I got to learn about the roots of Black Music

89
00:07:48,885 --> 00:07:52,889
At the end, I wanted to check the music scene from Rio de Janeiro

90
00:07:52,889 --> 00:07:56,309
There I made a lot of friends

91
00:07:56,309 --> 00:07:58,853
I was playing music almost every day

92
00:07:58,853 --> 00:08:02,023
That "Brazilian Latin Vibe"

93
00:08:02,023 --> 00:08:06,819
Become second nature for me due to the training sessions

94
00:08:06,819 --> 00:08:08,237
It was this kind of trip.

95
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:37,683
Haruomi Hosono

96
00:08:37,683 --> 00:08:42,855
He was part of a band called  "Happy Ending"

97
00:08:42,855 --> 00:08:46,984
A long ago the most famouband was Yellow Magic Orchestra

98
00:08:46,984 --> 00:08:49,779
They played in many places

99
00:08:49,779 --> 00:08:52,573
including The Greek Theatre in LA

100
00:08:53,533 --> 00:08:59,288
Ken is a great bassist, or rather, a great musician

101
00:08:59,288 --> 00:09:01,874
The songs are really good too,

102
00:09:01,874 --> 00:09:04,585
Recently he released an album

103
00:09:05,294 --> 00:09:06,754
That was really cool

104
00:09:07,129 --> 00:09:08,798
Kosugi Takehisa

105
00:09:09,590 --> 00:09:16,472
He's best known for the band "Taj Mahal Travellers"

106
00:09:16,681 --> 00:09:20,810
Back in his student years, he was part of Group Ongaku

107
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:23,646
It was an improvisational music group.

108
00:09:23,729 --> 00:09:27,233
Their music

109
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:29,777
is something that we adopted

110
00:09:29,777 --> 00:09:33,239
I have a lot of respect for him.

111
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:38,911
He's not as well-known as Haruomi Hosono

112
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:45,876
He doesn't make the kind of music most people listen to,

113
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:49,547
The relationship between people

114
00:09:49,672 --> 00:09:52,383
or the relationship between improvisation and drones

115
00:09:52,591 --> 00:09:59,181
I consider him to be the one that has explored such things most thoroughly in Japan's history

116
00:09:59,181 --> 00:10:02,893
His essasys, his writings

117
00:10:02,893 --> 00:10:05,563
his music at Taj Mahal Travellers

118
00:10:05,563 --> 00:10:10,318
I repesect everything about the way he lives his life.

119
00:10:10,401 --> 00:10:13,362
Shintaro Sakamoto

120
00:10:13,612 --> 00:10:20,119
Yurayura Teikoku 

121
00:10:20,119 --> 00:10:26,625
I think they're the most alternative band in Japan.

